<div id="p4" class="ltx_para ltx_align_center">
<p id="p4.1" class="ltx_p"><sup id="p4.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">??</span></sup><span id="p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;"> Université Paris Nanterre , <span id="p4.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_upright">??</span></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the Ahlswede–Daykin four-function theorem with a more recent four-function theorem due to Cordero-Erausquin and Maurey <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> that resembles the classical Prékopa–Leindler inequality. The two theorems have a common form, with the coordinatewise minimum and maximum in the former replaced by complementary arithmetic means in the latter. In exploring this analogy, we give transport proofs of both theorems and obtain extensions involving nonlinear means.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">More precisely, define the generalized mean, for <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{R}\cup\{\pm\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{R}\cup\{\pm\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math>, by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\alpha}^{\lambda}(x,y):=\left(\lambda x^{\alpha}+(1-\lambda)y^{\alpha}\right)^{1/\alpha}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\alpha}^{\lambda}(x,y):=\left(\lambda x^{\alpha}+(1-\lambda)y^{\alpha}\right)^{1/\alpha},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the special cases <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0,\pm\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0,\pm\infty</annotation></semantics></math> are understood in the limit:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{0}^{\lambda}(x,y)=x^{\lambda}y^{1-\lambda},\qquad M_{-\infty}^{\lambda}(x,y)=\min(x,y),\qquad M_{\infty}^{\lambda}(x,y)=\max(x,y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{0}^{\lambda}(x,y)=x^{\lambda}y^{1-\lambda},\qquad M_{-\infty}^{\lambda}(x,y)=\min(x,y),\qquad M_{\infty}^{\lambda}(x,y)=\max(x,y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">For vectors, the means act coordinatewise. On the positive orthant, the continuous version of the Ahlswede–Daykin theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> due to Batty and Bollmann <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> gives
<math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int f_{1}\int f_{2}\leq\int f_{3}\int f_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int f_{1}\int f_{2}\leq\int f_{3}\int f_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> under the assumption</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(M_{-\infty}^{\lambda}(x,y))f_{4}(M_{\infty}^{1-\lambda}(x,y))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(M_{-\infty}^{\lambda}(x,y))f_{4}(M_{\infty}^{1-\lambda}(x,y)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">while Cordero-Erausquin and Maurey proved, among other things, the same conclusion under the assumption</p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(M_{1}^{\lambda}(x,y))f_{4}(M_{1}^{1-\lambda}(x,y))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(M_{1}^{\lambda}(x,y))f_{4}(M_{1}^{1-\lambda}(x,y)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Here the functions are nonnegative, measurable and integrable; see Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 7.2 (Ahlswede &amp; Daykin, Cordero-Erausquin &amp; Maurey). ‣ 7.1 The four-function inequalities of Ahlswede–Daykin and Cordero-Erausquin–Maurey ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a> for a precise statement and the relation to the corresponding results on <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. The assumptions are therefore both of the form</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(M_{\alpha}^{\lambda}(x,y))f_{4}(M_{\beta}^{1-\lambda}(x,y))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>β</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(M_{\alpha}^{\lambda}(x,y))f_{4}(M_{\beta}^{1-\lambda}(x,y)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.6" class="ltx_p">It is natural to ask which other pairs of means give the same integral inequality. For equal weights, we obtain the following sharp range.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\alpha+\beta\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\alpha+\beta\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. If nonnegative measurable integrable functions <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1},f_{2},f_{3},f_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1},f_{2},f_{3},f_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,\infty)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,\infty)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(M_{\alpha}^{1/2}(x,y))f_{4}(M_{\beta}^{1/2}(x,y))\qquad\text{for all }x,y\in(0,\infty)^{d}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>β</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(M_{\alpha}^{1/2}(x,y))f_{4}(M_{\beta}^{1/2}(x,y))\qquad\text{for all }x,y\in(0,\infty)^{d},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\int f_{1}\right)\left(\int f_{2}\right)\leq\left(\int f_{3}\right)\left(\int f_{4}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\int f_{1}\right)\left(\int f_{2}\right)\leq\left(\int f_{3}\right)\left(\int f_{4}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where all integrals are over <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,\infty)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,\infty)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. The range <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\alpha+\beta\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\alpha+\beta\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> is necessary for this implication to hold for all such functions, already in dimension one.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This is the functional assertion of Corollary <a href="#S7.Thmthm11" title="Corollary 7.11 (Equal-weight power means). ‣ 7.3 Power-mean consequences ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.11</span></a>. The range includes all complementary pairs <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,1-q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,1-q)</annotation></semantics></math> and reaches the Ahlswede–Daykin inequality in the min–max limit. For unequal weights <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, our transport approach also gives the integral inequality for <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> (Corollary <a href="#S7.Thmthm8" title="Corollary 7.8 (Weighted four-function inequality). ‣ 7.3 Power-mean consequences ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.8</span></a>), extending <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> to nonlinear conditions and answering the authors’ question about a transport proof of their result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The proofs proceed through entropy inequalities under the quadratic optimal transport coupling, following the approach of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. A particularly simple case is the min–max inequality</p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(X\wedge Y)+H(X\vee Y)\geq H(X)+H(Y)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X\wedge Y)+H(X\vee Y)\geq H(X)+H(Y),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,Y</annotation></semantics></math> have absolutely continuous laws with finite second moments and finite Shannon entropies, and are coupled by quadratic optimal transport (Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3 The Euclidean Ahlswede–Daykin 4-function theorem ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>). Thus</p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(X\wedge Y)N(X\vee Y)\geq N(X)N(Y)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(X\wedge Y)N(X\vee Y)\geq N(X)N(Y),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(X)=e^{2H(X)/d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(X)=e^{2H(X)/d}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is an entropy power inequality for the join and meet in Euclidean space, but under the optimal transport coupling rather than the independent coupling. More generally, a scalar Jacobian criterion gives entropy inequalities for coordinatewise power means in arbitrary dimension (Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 7.6 (A scalar criterion for power-mean inequalities). ‣ 7.2 A scalar criterion and its transport proof ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.6</span></a>). The functional inequalities follow by the variational formula for entropy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We also study the stability of four-function inequalities under convolution, motivated by a recent investigation of super-additivity properties of entropy for dependent random variables <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. It has long been known that the convolution of two log-supermodular densities need not be log-supermodular <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, p. 486]</cite>. We show that convolution with a log-concave product density preserves four-function inequalities. When the four functions coincide, this recovers the preservation of log-supermodularity established by Karlin and Rinott <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, Proposition 3.7]</cite>, which in particular answers the question about Gaussian convolution raised by Zartash and Robeva <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Combining this preservation property with the results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> gives a conditional entropy power inequality. Namely, if <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,Y</annotation></semantics></math> are real-valued random variables with log-supermodular joint density <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}|X|^{2}+\mathbb{E}|Y|^{2}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}|X|^{2}+\mathbb{E}|Y|^{2}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\log p\in L^{1}(\mathbb{R}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\log p\in L^{1}(\mathbb{R}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{2H(X+Y)}\geq e^{2H(X\mid Y)}+e^{2H(Y\mid X)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{2H(X+Y)}\geq e^{2H(X\mid Y)}+e^{2H(Y\mid X)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(X\mid Y)=H(X,Y)-H(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X\mid Y)=H(X,Y)-H(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> are independent, this reduces to the classical entropy power inequality of Shannon.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Let us outline what remains. In Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Definitions ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> we give definitions, notation, and a bit of background. Section <a href="#S3" title="3 The Euclidean Ahlswede–Daykin 4-function theorem ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> studies the Euclidean Ahlswede–Daykin theorem and its entropic analog. We obtain the Riemann-integrable case from the finite-lattice theorem by truncation and Riemann sums, and give an entropy argument for general integrable functions. Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Stability of Convolution ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> establishes preservation of four-function inequalities under convolution with log-concave product densities on <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Log-supermodularity and entropy power inequality ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> explains the application to the conditional entropy power inequality. In Section <a href="#S6" title="6 A transport proof of the min–max entropy inequality ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> we prove the min–max entropy inequality by transport. We conclude in Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> with the scalar Jacobian criterion and its weighted and equal-weight power-mean consequences.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Definitions</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When a non-negative function <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, we will say it is a density. If <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int f(z)dz=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int f(z)dz=1</annotation></semantics></math>, we will call <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> a probability density. We will abbreviate when convenient the Lebesgue integral of a function <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}f(z)dz" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}f(z)dz</annotation></semantics></math> by just <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int f</annotation></semantics></math>. A function <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave when <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> imply that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f((1-t)x+ty)\geq f^{1-t}(x)f^{t}(y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f((1-t)x+ty)\geq f^{1-t}(x)f^{t}(y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We will call a density <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> a product density if it admits a decomposition <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(z)=\prod_{i=1}^{d}f_{i}(z_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(z)=\prod_{i=1}^{d}f_{i}(z_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:\mathbb{R}\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:\mathbb{R}\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A function <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\colon\mathbb{R}^{d}\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\colon\mathbb{R}^{d}\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be log-supermodular if for all <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(x_{1},\dots,x_{d}),y=(y_{1},\dots,y_{d})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(x_{1},\dots,x_{d}),y=(y_{1},\dots,y_{d})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> it holds</p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(x)u(y)\leq u(x\wedge y)u(x\vee y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(x)u(y)\leq u(x\wedge y)u(x\vee y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where
<math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\vee y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\vee y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the componentwise maximum of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\wedge y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\wedge y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>
denotes the componentwise minimum of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. Namely, if <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(x_{1},\dots,x_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(x_{1},\dots,x_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=(y_{1},\dots,y_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=(y_{1},\dots,y_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x\wedge y)_{i}=\min(x_{i},y_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x\wedge y)_{i}=\min(x_{i},y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x\vee y)_{i}=\max(x_{i},y_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x\vee y)_{i}=\max(x_{i},y_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Densities of this form have been widely studied in
various fields of mathematics– they are sometimes referred to as MTP<sub id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sub">2</sub> (multivariate totally positive of order 2). The introduction of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> provides a review of the literature.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly positive, log-supermodularity of <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to submodularity of the potential <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\coloneqq-\log u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\coloneqq-\log u</annotation></semantics></math>, in the sense that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(x)+V(y)\geq V(x\wedge y)+V(x\vee y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(x)+V(y)\geq V(x\wedge y)+V(x\vee y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Submodular functions have been extensively studied in combinatorial optimization (see, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>) and are of importance to information theory (see, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>),
and more recently have found use in convex geometry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For an Abelian group <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with Haar measure <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dz" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dz</annotation></semantics></math>, and functions <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f,g:G\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f,g:G\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> we write the usual convolution as <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f*g(x)\coloneqq\int_{G}f(x-z)g(z)dz" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f*g(x)\coloneqq\int_{G}f(x-z)g(z)dz</annotation></semantics></math> whenever this integral is well defined. When the significance is clear from context, we will abbreviate integrals by <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int f\coloneqq\int_{G}f(z)dz" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int f\coloneqq\int_{G}f(z)dz</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We write <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> for the probability measures with finite second moment and <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for the quadratic Wasserstein distance:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}(\mu,\nu)^{2}=\inf_{\pi\in\Pi(\mu,\nu)}\int|x-y|^{2}\,d\pi(x,y)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}(\mu,\nu)^{2}=\inf_{\pi\in\Pi(\mu,\nu)}\int|x-y|^{2}\,d\pi(x,y),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi(\mu,\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi(\mu,\nu)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of couplings of <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>. For a measurable map <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\#}\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\#}\mu</annotation></semantics></math> denotes its pushforward of <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let us also record the entropy conventions used throughout. In an
expression of the form <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\mu\mid\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mu\mid\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> denotes Lebesgue
measure on the relevant Euclidean space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a random variable <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with probability density function <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> we denote the Shannon entropy of <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H(X)\coloneqq-\int f\log f" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≔</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>f</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X)\coloneqq-\int f\log f</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">provided the integral is well defined.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a random variable <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> with well-defined Shannon entropy,
its entropy power is</p>
<table id="S2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(X)\coloneqq e^{\frac{2H(X)}{d}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(X)\coloneqq e^{\frac{2H(X)}{d}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, if <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a real-valued random variable, <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is just <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{2H(X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{2H(X)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> be a probability measure and let <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-finite
reference measure on the same measurable space. If <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\ll\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\ll\rho</annotation></semantics></math> and the
extended integral below is well defined, the relative entropy of <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to
<math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S2.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\mu\mid\rho)\coloneqq\int\log\left(\frac{d\mu}{d\rho}\right)\,d\mu." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mu\mid\rho)\coloneqq\int\log\left(\frac{d\mu}{d\rho}\right)\,d\mu.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We set <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\mu\mid\rho)=+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mu\mid\rho)=+\infty</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\not\ll\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≪̸</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\not\ll\rho</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is a
probability measure, this is the usual nonnegative relative entropy. When
<math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is Lebesgue measure, it need not be nonnegative. In
particular, if <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=f\,d\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=f\,d\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is the law of <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\mu\mid\lambda)=\int f\log f\,d\lambda=-H(X)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mu\mid\lambda)=\int f\log f\,d\lambda=-H(X),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">whenever these quantities are defined.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>The Euclidean Ahlswede–Daykin 4-function theorem</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The original four-function theorem of Ahlswede and Daykin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> is classically stated on a finite distributive lattice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Ahlswede–Daykin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If nonnegative functions <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> on a finite distributive lattice satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(x\wedge y)f_{4}(x\vee y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(x\wedge y)f_{4}(x\vee y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int f_{1}\int f_{2}\leq\int f_{3}\int f_{4}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int f_{1}\int f_{2}\leq\int f_{3}\int f_{4},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes integration with respect to the counting measure.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> for more background on the inequality’s significance and utility. There is also a <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m</annotation></semantics></math>-function generalization of the Ahlswede–Daykin theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> (see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>); a different extension is considered in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the celebrated FKG inequality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> is a special case of the Ahlswede–Daykin theorem. Indeed, if <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b</annotation></semantics></math> are nonnegative and increasing and <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is log-supermodular, apply the theorem with
<math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}=a\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}=a\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{2}=b\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{2}=b\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{3}=\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{3}=\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{4}=ab\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{4}=ab\mu</annotation></semantics></math>. On a finite lattice, general increasing functions can be shifted by constants to be nonnegative, without changing their covariance.
The continuous version of the FKG inequality was developed a long time ago <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> (see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> where extensions to countable product spaces as well as various applications are detailed).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We will have use for the following Euclidean version, due to Batty and Bollmann <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, of the four-function theorem of Ahlswede and Daykin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Batty-Bollmann <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmthm2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> functions <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
