<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is integration with respect to the usual Lebesgue measure.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">A new and self-contained transport proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 3.2 (Batty-Bollmann []). ‣ 3 The Euclidean Ahlswede–Daykin 4-function theorem ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> is obtained below using the entropy inequality proved in Section <a href="#S6" title="6 A transport proof of the min–max entropy inequality ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
First we show, in the case that the <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are Riemann integrable, how Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 3.2 (Batty-Bollmann []). ‣ 3 The Euclidean Ahlswede–Daykin 4-function theorem ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> follows from the finite version by truncation and convergence of Riemann sums.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Sketch of proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 3.2 (Batty-Bollmann []). ‣ 3 The Euclidean Ahlswede–Daykin 4-function theorem ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and, for <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, set <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{N}=n/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{N}=n/N</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{N}=\varepsilon_{N}\mathbb{Z}^{d}\cap[-n,n]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{N}=\varepsilon_{N}\mathbb{Z}^{d}\cap[-n,n]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. The restrictions
<math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{f}_{i}=f_{i}|_{L_{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{f}_{i}=f_{i}|_{L_{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{f}_{1}(x)\widetilde{f}_{2}(y)\leq\widetilde{f}_{3}(x\wedge y)\widetilde{f}_{4}(x\vee y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{f}_{1}(x)\widetilde{f}_{2}(y)\leq\widetilde{f}_{3}(x\wedge y)\widetilde{f}_{4}(x\vee y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">on the finite distributive lattice <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence Ahlswede–Daykin gives</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\sum_{x\in L_{N}}\widetilde{f}_{1}(x)\right)\left(\sum_{x\in L_{N}}\widetilde{f}_{2}(x)\right)\leq\left(\sum_{x\in L_{N}}\widetilde{f}_{3}(x)\right)\left(\sum_{x\in L_{N}}\widetilde{f}_{4}(x)\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\sum_{x\in L_{N}}\widetilde{f}_{1}(x)\right)\left(\sum_{x\in L_{N}}\widetilde{f}_{2}(x)\right)\leq\left(\sum_{x\in L_{N}}\widetilde{f}_{3}(x)\right)\left(\sum_{x\in L_{N}}\widetilde{f}_{4}(x)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">Multiply by <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{N}^{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{N}^{2d}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Riemann
integrability on <math id="S3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-n,n]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-n,n]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> gives</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\mathbbm{1}_{[-n,n]^{d}}f_{1}\int\mathbbm{1}_{[-n,n]^{d}}f_{2}\leq\int\mathbbm{1}_{[-n,n]^{d}}f_{3}\int\mathbbm{1}_{[-n,n]^{d}}f_{4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\mathbbm{1}_{[-n,n]^{d}}f_{1}\int\mathbbm{1}_{[-n,n]^{d}}f_{2}\leq\int\mathbbm{1}_{[-n,n]^{d}}f_{3}\int\mathbbm{1}_{[-n,n]^{d}}f_{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text">Since the truncation sets increase to <math id="S3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, monotone
convergence as <math id="S3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> proves the theorem.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, who pioneered this approach in a discrete setting, we now give a proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 3.2 (Batty-Bollmann []). ‣ 3 The Euclidean Ahlswede–Daykin 4-function theorem ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> via
a new entropy inequality for coordinatewise minimum and maximum. First we state this entropy inequality, which we will prove in Section <a href="#S6" title="6 A transport proof of the min–max entropy inequality ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>
via an optimal transport argument.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{1},\nu_{2}\in\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{1},\nu_{2}\in\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> be absolutely continuous probability measures. Let <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> be their quadratic optimal transport coupling. Since <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is absolutely continuous, there exists a Brenier map</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\nabla\varphi" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\nabla\varphi</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=(\operatorname{Id},T)_{\#}\nu_{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Id</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=(\operatorname{Id},T)_{\#}\nu_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Define</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{-}(x,y)=x\wedge y,\qquad m_{+}(x,y)=x\vee y," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{-}(x,y)=x\wedge y,\qquad m_{+}(x,y)=x\vee y,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the minimum and maximum are taken coordinatewise, and set</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{-}=(m_{-})_{\#}\pi,\qquad\nu_{+}=(m_{+})_{\#}\pi." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{-}=(m_{-})_{\#}\pi,\qquad\nu_{+}=(m_{+})_{\#}\pi.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then</span></p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\nu_{-}\mid\lambda)+H(\nu_{+}\mid\lambda)\leq H(\nu_{1}\mid\lambda)+H(\nu_{2}\mid\lambda)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\nu_{-}\mid\lambda)+H(\nu_{+}\mid\lambda)\leq H(\nu_{1}\mid\lambda)+H(\nu_{2}\mid\lambda),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with values allowed in <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(-\infty,+\infty]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-\infty,+\infty]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math> have the quadratic optimal transport coupling <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> from
Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3 The Euclidean Ahlswede–Daykin 4-function theorem ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>. In terms of Shannon entropy,
(<a href="#S3.E2" title="In Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3 The Euclidean Ahlswede–Daykin 4-function theorem ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is</p>
<table id="S3.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(X\wedge Y)+H(X\vee Y)\geq H(X)+H(Y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X\wedge Y)+H(X\vee Y)\geq H(X)+H(Y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Consequently, it is equivalent to the multiplicative
entropy-power inequality</p>
<table id="S3.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(X\wedge Y)N(X\vee Y)\geq N(X)N(Y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(X\wedge Y)N(X\vee Y)\geq N(X)N(Y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">It is in this sense that Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3 The Euclidean Ahlswede–Daykin 4-function theorem ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> may be
viewed as an entropy power inequality for minimum and maximum. Unlike the
classical entropy power inequality, however, it concerns the optimal
transport coupling rather than the independent coupling. More broadly,
submodularity and supermodularity properties of information-theoretic
functionals under natural operations have proved useful elsewhere. For
example, the submodularity of joint entropy was essentially known to Shannon (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> for an
explicit statement), and that of entropy under convolution was established in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> (see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> for the locally compact abelian-group setting).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 3.2 (Batty-Bollmann []). ‣ 3 The Euclidean Ahlswede–Daykin 4-function theorem ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1},f_{2},f_{3},f_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1},f_{2},f_{3},f_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> be functions satisfying the hypothesis of the theorem. If <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int f_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int f_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int f_{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int f_{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the conclusion is immediate. If both integrals are positive, then so are <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int f_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int f_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int f_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int f_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>: indeed, partition a positive-measure subset of <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{f_{1}&gt;0\}\times\{f_{2}&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{1}&gt;0\}\times\{f_{2}&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math> according to the coordinatewise order of <math id="S3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>. On at least one of the resulting <math id="S3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> pieces, the map</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\longmapsto(x\wedge y,x\vee y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\longmapsto(x\wedge y,x\vee y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">is a coordinate permutation and the piece has positive measure. The pointwise hypothesis and Fubini’s theorem then imply that both <math id="S3.p8.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{f_{3}&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{3}&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p8.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{f_{4}&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{4}&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math> have positive measure.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{1},\nu_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{1},\nu_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be compactly supported absolutely continuous probability measures of finite entropy, concentrated respectively on <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{f_{1}&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{1}&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{f_{2}&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{2}&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and such that <math id="S3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log f_{i}\in L^{1}(\nu_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log f_{i}\in L^{1}(\nu_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> be their quadratic optimal coupling and define <math id="S3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{-},\nu_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{-},\nu_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> as in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3 The Euclidean Ahlswede–Daykin 4-function theorem ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.
The output measures have compact support and finite entropy by Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3 The Euclidean Ahlswede–Daykin 4-function theorem ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>. The Gibbs bound on a compact set containing their supports, applied to the bounded potentials <math id="S3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\{\log(1+f_{j}),k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\{\log(1+f_{j}),k\}</annotation></semantics></math> and then letting <math id="S3.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, gives integrability of the positive parts of their logarithms. The pointwise hypothesis and the integrability of the input logarithms then give integrability of the negative parts. Thus all logarithmic integrals below are finite.
Then, by definition of the coupling <math id="S3.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, by hypothesis and by definition of <math id="S3.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{-},\nu_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{-},\nu_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int\log f_{1}d\nu_{1}+\int\log f_{2}d\nu_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int\log f_{1}d\nu_{1}+\int\log f_{2}d\nu_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\iint\log f_{1}(x)+\log f_{2}(y)\pi(dx,dy)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>∬</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\iint\log f_{1}(x)+\log f_{2}(y)\pi(dx,dy)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\iint\log f_{3}(m_{-}(x,y))+\log f_{4}(m_{+}(x,y))\pi(dx,dy)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>∬</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\iint\log f_{3}(m_{-}(x,y))+\log f_{4}(m_{+}(x,y))\pi(dx,dy)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int\log f_{3}d\nu_{-}+\int\log f_{4}d\nu_{+}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int\log f_{3}d\nu_{-}+\int\log f_{4}d\nu_{+}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">Subtracting the inequality of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3 The Euclidean Ahlswede–Daykin 4-function theorem ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, we end up with</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex37"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left[\int\log f_{1}d\nu_{1}-H(\nu_{1}|\lambda)\right]+\left[\int\log f_{2}d\nu_{2}-H(\nu_{2}|\lambda)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left[\int\log f_{1}d\nu_{1}-H(\nu_{1}|\lambda)\right]+\left[\int\log f_{2}d\nu_{2}-H(\nu_{2}|\lambda)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\left[\int\log f_{3}d\nu_{-}-H(\nu_{-}|\lambda)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\left[\int\log f_{3}d\nu_{-}-H(\nu_{-}|\lambda)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex38"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+\left[\int\log f_{4}d\nu_{+}-H(\nu_{+}|\lambda)\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+\left[\int\log f_{4}d\nu_{+}-H(\nu_{+}|\lambda)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.3.1" class="ltx_text">We use the following dual expression of the log-Laplace transform, for a measurable potential <math id="S3.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\int e^{\varphi}\,d\lambda&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.111em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>φ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\int e^{\varphi}\,d\lambda&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\log\int e^{\varphi}\,d\lambda=\sup_{\nu}\left\{\int\varphi\,d\nu-H(\nu\mid\lambda)\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>φ</mi></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mi>ν</mi></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><mi>φ</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\int e^{\varphi}\,d\lambda=\sup_{\nu}\left\{\int\varphi\,d\nu-H(\nu\mid\lambda)\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.4.1" class="ltx_text">where the supremum is over absolutely continuous probability measures with compact support, finite entropy relative to <math id="S3.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\in L^{1}(\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\in L^{1}(\nu)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that although the Donsker-Varadhan variational formula <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Lemma 2.1]</cite> was originally stated for probability measures,
it implies the corresponding statement for <math id="S3.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-finite reference measures as follows. Set</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=\int e^{\varphi}\,d\lambda,\qquad d\mu_{\varphi}=Z^{-1}e^{\varphi}\,d\lambda." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>φ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>φ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=\int e^{\varphi}\,d\lambda,\qquad d\mu_{\varphi}=Z^{-1}e^{\varphi}\,d\lambda.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.5.1" class="ltx_text">For every admissible <math id="S3.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, the identity</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\nu\mid\mu_{\varphi})=H(\nu\mid\lambda)-\int\varphi\,d\nu+\log Z," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\nu\mid\mu_{\varphi})=H(\nu\mid\lambda)-\int\varphi\,d\nu+\log Z,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.6.1" class="ltx_text">and the nonnegativity of relative entropy give</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\varphi\,d\nu-H(\nu\mid\lambda)\leq\log Z." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\varphi\,d\nu-H(\nu\mid\lambda)\leq\log Z.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.7.1" class="ltx_text">For the reverse bound, put <math id="S3.p9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=e^{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>φ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=e^{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> and set</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{k}=\{x\in[-k,k]^{d}:k^{-1}\leq f(x)\leq k\},\qquad Z_{k}=\int_{E_{k}}f\,d\lambda." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{k}=\{x\in[-k,k]^{d}:k^{-1}\leq f(x)\leq k\},\qquad Z_{k}=\int_{E_{k}}f\,d\lambda.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.8.1" class="ltx_text">For every <math id="S3.p9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p9.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the probabilities <math id="S3.p9.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\nu_{k}=Z_{k}^{-1}f\mathbbm{1}_{E_{k}}\,d\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\nu_{k}=Z_{k}^{-1}f\mathbbm{1}_{E_{k}}\,d\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> are admissible and satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\int\log f\,d\nu_{k}-H(\nu_{k}\mid\lambda)=\log Z_{k}\longrightarrow\log\int f\,d\lambda." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>∫</mo><mi>f</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\log f\,d\nu_{k}-H(\nu_{k}\mid\lambda)=\log Z_{k}\longrightarrow\log\int f\,d\lambda.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.9.1" class="ltx_text">The two terms on the right of the entropy inequality are bounded by
<math id="S3.p9.m22" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\log\int f_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>∫</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\int f_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p9.m23" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\log\int f_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>∫</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\int f_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Optimizing its left
side independently over <math id="S3.p9.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p9.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> therefore gives</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\log\int f_{1}+\log\int f_{2}\leq\log\int f_{3}+\log\int f_{4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>∫</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>∫</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>∫</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>∫</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\int f_{1}+\log\int f_{2}\leq\log\int f_{3}+\log\int f_{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.10" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.10.1" class="ltx_text">Exponentiating proves the claimed four-function inequality.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Stability of Convolution</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following lemma uses Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 3.2 (Batty-Bollmann []). ‣ 3 The Euclidean Ahlswede–Daykin 4-function theorem ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> to provide sufficient conditions for a density function to preserve log-supermodularity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let a density <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> be such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x-u)g(y-w)\leq g(x\wedge y-u\wedge w)g(x\vee y-u\vee w)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>∨</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x-u)g(y-w)\leq g(x\wedge y-u\wedge w)g(x\vee y-u\vee w),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y,u,w\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y,u,w\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,3,4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,3,4</annotation></semantics></math>, are measurable and integrable, each <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}*g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}*g</annotation></semantics></math> is finite everywhere, and</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(x\wedge y)f_{4}(x\vee y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(x\wedge y)f_{4}(x\vee y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y</annotation></semantics></math> then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}*g(x)\ f_{2}*g(y)\leq f_{3}*g(x\wedge y)\ f_{4}*g(x\vee y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}*g(x)\ f_{2}*g(y)\leq f_{3}*g(x\wedge y)\ f_{4}*g(x\vee y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular if <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is a log-supermodular density and <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f*g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f*g</annotation></semantics></math> is finite everywhere, then <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f*g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f*g</annotation></semantics></math> is log-supermodular.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and define</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex46"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{f}_{1}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{f}_{1}(z)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq f_{1}(z)g(x-z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq f_{1}(z)g(x-z)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex47"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{f}_{2}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{f}_{2}(z)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex47.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq f_{2}(z)g(y-z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq f_{2}(z)g(y-z)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{f}_{3}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{f}_{3}(z)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex48.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq f_{3}(z)g(x\wedge y-z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq f_{3}(z)g(x\wedge y-z)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex49"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{f}_{4}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{f}_{4}(z)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex49.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq f_{4}(z)g(x\vee y-z)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq f_{4}(z)g(x\vee y-z).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">We wish to show that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}*g(x)\ f_{2}*g(y)\leq f_{3}*g(x\wedge y)\ f_{4}*g(x\vee y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}*g(x)\ f_{2}*g(y)\leq f_{3}*g(x\wedge y)\ f_{4}*g(x\vee y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">which precisely means</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\tilde{f}_{1}\int\tilde{f}_{2}\leq\int\tilde{f}_{3}\int\tilde{f}_{4}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\tilde{f}_{1}\int\tilde{f}_{2}\leq\int\tilde{f}_{3}\int\tilde{f}_{4},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">so that by Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 3.2 (Batty-Bollmann []). ‣ 3 The Euclidean Ahlswede–Daykin 4-function theorem ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> it suffices to check that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}_{1}(u)\tilde{f}_{2}(w)\leq\tilde{f}_{3}(u\wedge w)\tilde{f}_{4}(u\vee w)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}_{1}(u)\tilde{f}_{2}(w)\leq\tilde{f}_{3}(u\wedge w)\tilde{f}_{4}(u\vee w).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.5.1" class="ltx_text">This is exactly</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(u)g(x-u)f_{2}(w)g(y-w)\leq f_{3}(u\wedge w)g(x\wedge y-u\wedge w)f_{4}(u\vee w)g(x\vee y-u\vee w)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>∨</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(u)g(x-u)f_{2}(w)g(y-w)\leq f_{3}(u\wedge w)g(x\wedge y-u\wedge w)f_{4}(u\vee w)g(x\vee y-u\vee w),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.6.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, using (<a href="#S4.E5" title="In Lemma 4.1. ‣ 4 Stability of Convolution ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) the conclusion follows from</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x-u)g(y-w)\leq g(x\wedge y-u\wedge w)g(x\vee y-u\vee w)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>∨</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x-u)g(y-w)\leq g(x\wedge y-u\wedge w)g(x\vee y-u\vee w),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.7.1" class="ltx_text">which is exactly our hypothesis. Taking <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}=f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}=f</annotation></semantics></math> for a log-supermodular function, shows the stability of log-supermodularity under log-concave product convolutions.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that (<a href="#S4.E4" title="In Lemma 4.1. ‣ 4 Stability of Convolution ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) is equivalent to <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:(\mathbb{R}^{d})^{2}\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:(\mathbb{R}^{d})^{2}\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> defined by</p>
<table id="S4.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(x,y)=g(x-y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(x,y)=g(x-y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">being log-supermodular. Write <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\log g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\log g</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose for this heuristic that <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is positive and smooth. Applying the differentiable characterization of supermodularity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> to
<math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log u(x,y)=V(x-y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log u(x,y)=V(x-y)</annotation></semantics></math>, and differentiating in the directions <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{j}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{j}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>, gives</p>
<table id="S4.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial^{2}}{\partial x_{i}\partial{x_{j}}}\log u(x,y)=\frac{\partial^{2}V}{\partial x_{i}\partial x_{j}}\geq 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial^{2}}{\partial x_{i}\partial{x_{j}}}\log u(x,y)=\frac{\partial^{2}V}{\partial x_{i}\partial x_{j}}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.3" class="ltx_p">while differentiating in the directions <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{j}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{j}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> gives</p>
<table id="S4.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\frac{\partial^{2}V}{\partial x_{i}\partial x_{j}}\geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\frac{\partial^{2}V}{\partial x_{i}\partial x_{j}}\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.4" class="ltx_p">Thus the mixed partials of <math id="S4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> vanish. It follows that there exist <math id="S4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and a constant <math id="S4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(x)=c+V_{1}(x_{1})+\cdots+V_{d}(x_{d})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(x)=c+V_{1}(x_{1})+\cdots+V_{d}(x_{d}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.5" class="ltx_p">Moreover, differentiating in <math id="S4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> gives <math id="S4.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-V_{i}^{\prime\prime}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-V_{i}^{\prime\prime}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, so each <math id="S4.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is concave. This suggests that, at least as a consequence of Ahlswede–Daykin, <math id="S4.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> being a log-concave product density is a necessary condition for <math id="S4.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> to preserve inequality (<a href="#S4.E4" title="In Lemma 4.1. ‣ 4 Stability of Convolution ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) on convolution. The following result shows that this condition is sufficient.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> be a log-concave product density on <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,3,4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,3,4</annotation></semantics></math>, are measurable and integrable and satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex59"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(x\wedge y)f_{4}(x\vee y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(x\wedge y)f_{4}(x\vee y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y</annotation></semantics></math> then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}*g(x)\ f_{2}*g(y)\leq f_{3}*g(x\wedge y)\ f_{4}*g(x\vee y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}*g(x)\ f_{2}*g(y)\leq f_{3}*g(x\wedge y)\ f_{4}*g(x\vee y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that in the case all <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are equal to a single function <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, this recovers the classical result of Karlin-Rinott <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, Proposition 3.7]</cite> that log-concave<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          The Karlin-Rinott result is stated equivalently in terms of Pólya frequency functions, explicitly that log-concave densities coincide with the class PF<sub id="footnote1.2" class="ltx_sub">2</sub>.</span></span></span> product convolutions preserve log-supermodularity and answers a recent question of Zartash and Robeva <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since an integrable log-concave function on <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded, the product density <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded. Hence each <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}*g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}*g</annotation></semantics></math> is finite everywhere.
By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 4.1. ‣ 4 Stability of Convolution ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> it suffices to check (<a href="#S4.E4" title="In Lemma 4.1. ‣ 4 Stability of Convolution ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Since <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is a product density, given by <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(z)=\prod_{i=1}^{d}g_{i}(z_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(z)=\prod_{i=1}^{d}g_{i}(z_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> log-concave, (<a href="#S4.E4" title="In Lemma 4.1. ‣ 4 Stability of Convolution ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) is exactly</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{i=1}^{d}g_{i}(x_{i}-u_{i})g_{i}(y_{i}-w_{i})\leq\prod_{i=1}^{d}g_{i}(x_{i}\wedge y_{i}-u_{i}\wedge w_{i})g_{i}(x_{i}\vee y_{i}-u_{i}\vee w_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{i=1}^{d}g_{i}(x_{i}-u_{i})g_{i}(y_{i}-w_{i})\leq\prod_{i=1}^{d}g_{i}(x_{i}\wedge y_{i}-u_{i}\wedge w_{i})g_{i}(x_{i}\vee y_{i}-u_{i}\vee w_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Thus it suffices to prove (<a href="#S4.E4" title="In Lemma 4.1. ‣ 4 Stability of Convolution ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) when <math id="S4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is a log-concave density on <math id="S4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Write <math id="S4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=e^{-V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=e^{-V}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math> is convex; the value <math id="S4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+\infty</annotation></semantics></math> encodes the complement of the interval <math id="S4.p5.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{g&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is equivalent to prove</span></p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(x-u)+V(y-w)\geq V(x\wedge y-u\wedge w)+V(x\vee y-u\vee w)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>∨</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(x-u)+V(y-w)\geq V(x\wedge y-u\wedge w)+V(x\vee y-u\vee w).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">To this end, without loss of generality, we may assume <math id="S4.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\leq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\leq y</annotation></semantics></math> and note that if <math id="S4.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\leq w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\leq w</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S4.E6" title="In Proof. ‣ 4 Stability of Convolution ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) is equality. It remains to assume <math id="S4.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u&gt;w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u&gt;w</annotation></semantics></math> and thus prove</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(x-u)+V(y-w)\geq V(x-w)+V(y-u)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(x-u)+V(y-w)\geq V(x-w)+V(y-u).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="S4.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=x-u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=x-u</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=y-w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=y-w</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=x-w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=x-w</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=y-u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=y-u</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+b=c+d,\qquad|b-a|=(y-x)+(u-w)\geq|(y-x)-(u-w)|=|d-c|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+b=c+d,\qquad|b-a|=(y-x)+(u-w)\geq|(y-x)-(u-w)|=|d-c|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.5.1" class="ltx_text">Heuristically, for convex functions, spreading out points with the same sum increases the sum of their values. If either <math id="S4.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(a)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(b)</annotation></semantics></math> is infinite, the desired inequality is immediate. Otherwise, the displayed relations imply that there exists <math id="S4.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=\theta a+(1-\theta)b,\qquad d=(1-\theta)a+\theta b." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=\theta a+(1-\theta)b,\qquad d=(1-\theta)a+\theta b.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.6.1" class="ltx_text">Convexity therefore gives <math id="S4.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(c)+V(d)\leq V(a)+V(b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(c)+V(d)\leq V(a)+V(b)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the desired result.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Since the standard Gaussian is both log-concave and a product density, this yields the relevant stability under Gaussian convolution used in Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Corollary 5.3. ‣ 5 Log-supermodularity and entropy power inequality ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> the standard Gaussian density on <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> a log-supermodular density,
<math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f*g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f*g</annotation></semantics></math> is also log-supermodular.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">A scaling argument gives the corresponding statement for every centered
isotropic Gaussian density. Indeed, if <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is standard Gaussian and
<math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}(x)=t^{-d}g(x/t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}(x)=t^{-d}g(x/t)</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="S4.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f*g_{t})(x)=(\widetilde{f}*g)(x/t),\qquad\widetilde{f}(z)=f(tz)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f*g_{t})(x)=(\widetilde{f}*g)(x/t),\qquad\widetilde{f}(z)=f(tz),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.2" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is log-supermodular whenever <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is. Conversely, if
<math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f*g_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f*g_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is log-supermodular for every <math id="S4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f*g_{t}\to f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f*g_{t}\to f</annotation></semantics></math> at every
Lebesgue point of <math id="S4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. Passing to the limit in the defining inequality
gives</p>
<table id="S4.Ex66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)f(y)\leq f(x\wedge y)f(x\vee y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)f(y)\leq f(x\wedge y)f(x\vee y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.3" class="ltx_p">whenever all four displayed arguments are Lebesgue points. This holds
for almost every <math id="S4.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>: after partitioning according to the
coordinatewise order of <math id="S4.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, each of the four maps is a
coordinate projection, and the inverse image of a null set under such
a projection is null. Thus <math id="S4.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is log-supermodular in the almost
everywhere sense.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The same definitions apply on the <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional integer lattice. We consider <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{Z}^{d}\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{Z}^{d}\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> to be log-supermodular when <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)f(y)\leq f(x\wedge y)f(x\vee y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)f(y)\leq f(x\wedge y)f(x\vee y)</annotation></semantics></math> holds for all <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, considered as a subset of <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A function <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathbb{Z}\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathbb{Z}\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave if <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{g&gt;0\}=I\cap\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g&gt;0\}=I\cap\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> for some (possibly infinite) interval <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\subseteq\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\subseteq\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S4.Ex67" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{2}(n)\geq g(n+1)g(n-1)\qquad\qquad\forall n\in I\cap\mathbb{Z}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:4.167em;" width="4.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{2}(n)\geq g(n+1)g(n-1)\qquad\qquad\forall n\in I\cap\mathbb{Z}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">This definition on <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to the existence of a log-concave function on <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> interpolating <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, we will consider <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V:\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V:\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math> to be convex if there exists an extended-valued convex function on <math id="S4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> interpolating <math id="S4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">A function <math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathbb{Z}^{d}\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathbb{Z}^{d}\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> will be called a <math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> product density if it admits a decomposition into <math id="S4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> functions <math id="S4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}:\mathbb{Z}\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}:\mathbb{Z}\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex68" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(z)=\prod_{i=1}^{d}g_{i}(z_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(z)=\prod_{i=1}^{d}g_{i}(z_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathbb{Z}^{d}\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathbb{Z}^{d}\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> be summable and satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x-u)g(y-w)\leq g(x\wedge y-u\wedge w)g(x\vee y-u\vee w)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>∨</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x-u)g(y-w)\leq g(x\wedge y-u\wedge w)g(x\vee y-u\vee w),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y,u,w\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y,u,w\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:\mathbb{Z}^{d}\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:\mathbb{Z}^{d}\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> are summable and satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex69"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(x\wedge y)f_{4}(x\vee y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(x\wedge y)f_{4}(x\vee y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="S4.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y</annotation></semantics></math> then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex70" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}*g(x)\ f_{2}*g(y)\leq f_{3}*g(x\wedge y)\ f_{4}*g(x\vee y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}*g(x)\ f_{2}*g(y)\leq f_{3}*g(x\wedge y)\ f_{4}*g(x\vee y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof follows identically to Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 4.1. ‣ 4 Stability of Convolution ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> with sums replacing integrals.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(z)=\prod_{i=1}^{d}g_{i}(z_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(z)=\prod_{i=1}^{d}g_{i}(z_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> be a summable <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> product density whose factors <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are log-concave. If <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:\mathbb{Z}^{d}\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:\mathbb{Z}^{d}\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,3,4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,3,4</annotation></semantics></math>, are summable and</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex71" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(x\wedge y)f_{4}(x\vee y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(x\wedge y)f_{4}(x\vee y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex72" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}*g(x)f_{2}*g(y)\leq f_{3}*g(x\wedge y)\ f_{4}*g(x\vee y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}*g(x)f_{2}*g(y)\leq f_{3}*g(x\wedge y)\ f_{4}*g(x\vee y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="S4.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
