<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Log-supermodularity and entropy power inequality</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we sketch the connection between the stability of log-supermodularity under Gaussian convolution and a conditional entropy power inequality explored in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>.
We direct the interested reader to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, where we consider a collection of <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> random variables taking values in <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. For simplicity of exposition,
here we take random variables <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,Y)\in\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,Y)\in\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with a joint density <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and define the conditional entropy of <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex73" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(X|Y)\coloneqq H(X,Y)-H(Y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X|Y)\coloneqq H(X,Y)-H(Y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Typical regularity conditions are assumed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> and throughout this section to justify some exchange of limits and integration by parts (for the de Bruijn formula).
Namely we assume <math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}|X|^{2}+\mathbb{E}|Y|^{2}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}|X|^{2}+\mathbb{E}|Y|^{2}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and finite entropy, i.e., the joint density <math id="S5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\log p\in L_{1}(\mathbb{R}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\log p\in L_{1}(\mathbb{R}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We recall the entropy power inequality. Shannon formulated the inequality
in his seminal work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>; rigorous proofs were subsequently given
by Stam <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> and Blachman <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Entropy power inequality)<span id="S5.Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For independent <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>-valued random variables <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,Y</annotation></semantics></math> with densities and well-defined entropies <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(X),H(Y),H(X+Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X),H(Y),H(X+Y)</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex74" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(X+Y)\geq N(X)+N(Y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(X+Y)\geq N(X)+N(Y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">With the intention of extending some form of the entropy power inequality to dependent random variables, by studying the behavior of the conditional entropy, it will be convenient to define in the presence of a joint density for <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, a conditional entropy power,</p>
<table id="S5.Ex75" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex75.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(X|Y)\coloneqq e^{2H(X|Y)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(X|Y)\coloneqq e^{2H(X|Y)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> a (classical) semigroup approach is used to derive an integral inequality based on Fisher information bounds that extends previous work of Johnson <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.
In more detail, dropping the nonpositive terms involving differences between joint and marginal Fisher information in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, Corollary 5.2]</cite> gives the
following “conditional entropy power inequality”, with a deficit term controlled by an integral of a functional of the Fisher information. Set</p>
<table id="S5.Ex76" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\frac{N(X\mid Y)}{N(X\mid Y)+N(Y\mid X)}\in(0,1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\frac{N(X\mid Y)}{N(X\mid Y)+N(Y\mid X)}\in(0,1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be independent (of each other and of <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math>) standard Gaussian random variables and, for <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="S5.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{e^{-s}}{\sqrt{\lambda}}X+\sqrt{1-e^{-2s}}Z_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><msqrt><mi>λ</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{e^{-s}}{\sqrt{\lambda}}X+\sqrt{1-e^{-2s}}Z_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex77"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex77.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y_{s}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex77.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{e^{-s}}{\sqrt{1-\lambda}}Y+\sqrt{1-e^{-2s}}Z_{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{e^{-s}}{\sqrt{1-\lambda}}Y+\sqrt{1-e^{-2s}}Z_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p3.4" class="ltx_p">and let <math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the joint density of <math id="S5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{s},Y_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{s},Y_{s})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Madiman–Melbourne–Roberto)<span id="S5.Thmthm2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>-valued random variables <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> be as above. Then</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex78" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex78.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{S}\ N(X+Y)\geq N(X|Y)+N(Y|X)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>S</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{S}\ N(X+Y)\geq N(X|Y)+N(Y|X),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where in the notation of (<a href="#S5.E8" title="In 5 Log-supermodularity and entropy power inequality ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>),</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S\coloneqq 4\sqrt{\lambda(1-\lambda)}\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathbb{E}(\partial_{x}\log p_{s})(\partial_{y}\log p_{s})(X_{s},Y_{s})ds." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>y</mi></msub><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S\coloneqq 4\sqrt{\lambda(1-\lambda)}\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathbb{E}(\partial_{x}\log p_{s})(\partial_{y}\log p_{s})(X_{s},Y_{s})ds.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We obtain the conditional entropy power inequality stated in the introduction as a corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math> random variables with log-supermodular joint density,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex79" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex79.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(X+Y)\geq N(X|Y)+N(Y|X)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(X+Y)\geq N(X|Y)+N(Y|X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note that when <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math> are independent <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(X|Y)=N(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(X|Y)=N(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(Y|X)=N(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(Y|X)=N(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, one recovers Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 5.1 (Entropy power inequality). ‣ 5 Log-supermodularity and entropy power inequality ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We consider the integrand in (<a href="#S5.E9" title="In Theorem 5.2 (Madiman–Melbourne–Roberto). ‣ 5 Log-supermodularity and entropy power inequality ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) and integrate by parts,</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex80"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex80.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}(\partial_{x}\log p_{s})(\partial_{y}\log p_{s})(X_{s},Y_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>y</mi></msub><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}(\partial_{x}\log p_{s})(\partial_{y}\log p_{s})(X_{s},Y_{s})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex80.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{2}}(\partial_{x}\log p_{s})(\partial_{y}\log p_{s})p_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>y</mi></msub><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{2}}(\partial_{x}\log p_{s})(\partial_{y}\log p_{s})p_{s}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex81"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex81.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{2}}\partial_{x}p_{s}(\partial_{y}\log p_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>y</mi></msub><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{2}}\partial_{x}p_{s}(\partial_{y}\log p_{s})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex82"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex82.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\int_{\mathbb{R}^{2}}(\partial_{xy}\log p_{s})p_{s}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\int_{\mathbb{R}^{2}}(\partial_{xy}\log p_{s})p_{s}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, for any <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is log-supermodular, Topkis’ differentiable characterization gives <math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\partial_{xy}\log p_{s})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\partial_{xy}\log p_{s})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and hence the integrand is nonpositive. By a coordinatewise scaling argument, if <math id="S5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, the density of <math id="S5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, is log-supermodular, then for <math id="S5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> the density of</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex83" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex83.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\frac{e^{-s}}{\sqrt{\lambda}}X,\frac{e^{-s}}{\sqrt{1-\lambda}}Y\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><msqrt><mi>λ</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\frac{e^{-s}}{\sqrt{\lambda}}X,\frac{e^{-s}}{\sqrt{1-\lambda}}Y\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">is log-supermodular. Observing that <math id="S5.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{1-e^{-2s}}(Z_{1},Z_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{1-e^{-2s}}(Z_{1},Z_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> has a log-concave product density and applying Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4 Stability of Convolution ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, we have that <math id="S5.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, the density of</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex84" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex84.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{s},Y_{s})=\left(\frac{e^{-s}}{\sqrt{\lambda}}X,\frac{e^{-s}}{\sqrt{1-\lambda}}Y\right)+\sqrt{1-e^{-2s}}(Z_{1},Z_{2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><msqrt><mi>λ</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{s},Y_{s})=\left(\frac{e^{-s}}{\sqrt{\lambda}}X,\frac{e^{-s}}{\sqrt{1-\lambda}}Y\right)+\sqrt{1-e^{-2s}}(Z_{1},Z_{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p6.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text">is log-supermodular. Hence</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex85" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex85.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}(\partial_{x}\log p_{s})(\partial_{y}\log p_{s})(X_{s},Y_{s})\leq 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>y</mi></msub><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}(\partial_{x}\log p_{s})(\partial_{y}\log p_{s})(X_{s},Y_{s})\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p6.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.5.1" class="ltx_text">for every <math id="S5.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Integrating we have <math id="S5.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{S}\leq 1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>S</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{S}\leq 1,</annotation></semantics></math> completing the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>A transport proof of the min–max entropy inequality</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we give a transport proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 3.2 (Batty-Bollmann []). ‣ 3 The Euclidean Ahlswede–Daykin 4-function theorem ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first isolate some ingredients that will be used in the proof.
The first is the injectivity of min/max maps under strict monotonicity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\colon\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\colon\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be strictly monotone, in the sense that</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex86" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex86.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle T(x)-T(y),x-y\rangle&gt;0\qquad\text{whenever }x\neq y." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">whenever </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle T(x)-T(y),x-y\rangle&gt;0\qquad\text{whenever }x\neq y.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then the maps</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex87" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex87.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{-}(x)=x\wedge T(x),\qquad T_{+}(x)=x\vee T(x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{-}(x)=x\wedge T(x),\qquad T_{+}(x)=x\vee T(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">are injective.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose first that
<math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{-}(x)=T_{-}(y)=z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{-}(x)=T_{-}(y)=z</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since
<math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=x\wedge T(x)=y\wedge T(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=x\wedge T(x)=y\wedge T(y)</annotation></semantics></math>,
we have, coordinatewise,
<math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x-z\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x-z\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(x)-z\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(x)-z\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex88" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex88.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i}-z_{i})(T_{i}(x)-z_{i})=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i}-z_{i})(T_{i}(x)-z_{i})=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">for every <math id="S6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore,</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex89" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex89.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle x-z,T(x)-z\rangle=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle x-z,T(x)-z\rangle=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">The same identities hold with <math id="S6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> in place of <math id="S6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex90"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex90.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\langle x-y,T(x)-T(y)\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\langle x-y,T(x)-T(y)\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex90.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\big\langle(x-z)-(y-z),(T(x)-z)-(T(y)-z)\big\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\big\langle(x-z)-(y-z),(T(x)-z)-(T(y)-z)\big\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex91"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex91.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\langle x-z,T(y)-z\rangle-\langle y-z,T(x)-z\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\langle x-z,T(y)-z\rangle-\langle y-z,T(x)-z\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex92"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex92.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">Strict monotonicity implies <math id="S6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=y</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{-}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof for <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> is the same, with all coordinatewise inequalities
reversed.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We need a lemma allowing approximation by regular densities.
Let <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> denote the standard Gaussian probability measure on
<math id="S6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex93" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex93.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d\gamma(x)=(2\pi)^{-d/2}e^{-|x|^{2}/2}\,dx." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">−</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\gamma(x)=(2\pi)^{-d/2}e^{-|x|^{2}/2}\,dx.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.2</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu\in\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu\in\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\nu\mid\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\nu\mid\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> finite.
There exist bounded, smooth, uniformly convex domains <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and
probability measures <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ν</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> such that:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S6.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">each </span><math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ν</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu^{j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has a smooth density on </span><math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, bounded above and
below by positive constants;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu^{j}\longrightarrow\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu^{j}\longrightarrow\nu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in </span><math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\nu^{j}\mid\gamma)\longrightarrow H(\nu\mid\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ν</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\nu^{j}\mid\gamma)\longrightarrow H(\nu\mid\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S6.Thmthm2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any finite collection of measures, the approximations may be
chosen on the same domains <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Write <math id="S6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\nu=h\,d\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\nu=h\,d\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S6.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{R}=\nu(B_{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{R}=\nu(B_{R})</annotation></semantics></math> and define the normalized restriction</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex94" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex94.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\nu^{R}=\frac{\mathbbm{1}_{B_{R}}}{a_{R}}\,d\nu=\frac{\mathbbm{1}_{B_{R}}h}{a_{R}}\,d\gamma." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ν</mi><mi>R</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\nu^{R}=\frac{\mathbbm{1}_{B_{R}}}{a_{R}}\,d\nu=\frac{\mathbbm{1}_{B_{R}}h}{a_{R}}\,d\gamma.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S6.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu\in\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu\in\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S6.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu^{R}\longrightarrow\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu^{R}\longrightarrow\nu</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover,</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex95"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex95.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H(\nu^{R}\mid\gamma)=\int_{B_{R}}\frac{h}{a_{R}}\log\left(\frac{h}{a_{R}}\right)\,d\gamma=\frac{1}{a_{R}}\int_{B_{R}}h\log h\,d\gamma-\log a_{R}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ν</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H(\nu^{R}\mid\gamma)=\int_{B_{R}}\frac{h}{a_{R}}\log\left(\frac{h}{a_{R}}\right)\,d\gamma=\frac{1}{a_{R}}\int_{B_{R}}h\log h\,d\gamma-\log a_{R}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S6.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{R}\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{R}\to 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\log h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\log h</annotation></semantics></math> is integrable, it follows that</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex96" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex96.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\nu^{R}\mid\gamma)\longrightarrow H(\nu\mid\gamma)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ν</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\nu^{R}\mid\gamma)\longrightarrow H(\nu\mid\gamma).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix <math id="S6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and choose the larger ball</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex97" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex97.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=B_{R+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=B_{R+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">Write <math id="S6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=d\nu^{R}/d\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ν</mi><mi>R</mi></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=d\nu^{R}/d\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, extended by zero outside <math id="S6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. For
<math id="S6.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, set</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex98" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex98.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{M}=\frac{q\wedge M}{\int_{K}(q\wedge M)\,d\lambda}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{M}=\frac{q\wedge M}{\int_{K}(q\wedge M)\,d\lambda}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">Then <math id="S6.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{M}\to q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{M}\to q</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{1}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{1}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex99" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex99.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{K}q_{M}\log q_{M}\,d\lambda\longrightarrow\int_{K}q\log q\,d\lambda." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{K}q_{M}\log q_{M}\,d\lambda\longrightarrow\int_{K}q\log q\,d\lambda.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p7.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.4.1" class="ltx_text">Indeed, the positive part follows by monotone convergence before
normalization, while the negative part is dominated on the bounded
set <math id="S6.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> by the bound <math id="S6.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\log t\geq-e^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\log t\geq-e^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S6.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth mollifier supported in <math id="S6.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> and
choose <math id="S6.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. For fixed <math id="S6.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, the functions
<math id="S6.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{M,\delta}=q_{M}*\rho_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{M,\delta}=q_{M}*\rho_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> are supported in <math id="S6.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, converge to <math id="S6.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>
in <math id="S6.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and are uniformly bounded. Hence, as <math id="S6.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\downarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\downarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex100" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex100.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{K}g_{M,\delta}\log g_{M,\delta}\,d\lambda\longrightarrow\int_{K}q_{M}\log q_{M}\,d\lambda." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{K}g_{M,\delta}\log g_{M,\delta}\,d\lambda\longrightarrow\int_{K}q_{M}\log q_{M}\,d\lambda.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">For each <math id="S6.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, first choose <math id="S6.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{j}\geq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{j}\geq j</annotation></semantics></math> so that truncation changes both
the <math id="S6.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> distance and the entropy by at most <math id="S6.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then choose
<math id="S6.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\delta_{j}&lt;j^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\delta_{j}&lt;j^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> so that mollification contributes at most another
<math id="S6.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> to each error. The densities
<math id="S6.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{j}=g_{M_{j},\delta_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{j}=g_{M_{j},\delta_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> then converge to <math id="S6.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> both in <math id="S6.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{1}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{1}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> and in
Lebesgue entropy.
Replace <math id="S6.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> by</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex101" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex101.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{g}_{j}=(1-\varepsilon_{j})g_{j}+\frac{\varepsilon_{j}}{|K|},\qquad\varepsilon_{j}\downarrow 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{g}_{j}=(1-\varepsilon_{j})g_{j}+\frac{\varepsilon_{j}}{|K|},\qquad\varepsilon_{j}\downarrow 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">For fixed <math id="S6.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S6.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> change is at most <math id="S6.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\varepsilon_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\varepsilon_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the
entropy of <math id="S6.p8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{g}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{g}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> tends to that of <math id="S6.p8.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> as
<math id="S6.p8.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{j}\downarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{j}\downarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> by dominated convergence, since <math id="S6.p8.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is
bounded and <math id="S6.p8.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> has finite measure. We may therefore choose
<math id="S6.p8.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> so that each change is at most <math id="S6.p8.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S6.p8.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{g}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{g}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth on
<math id="S6.p8.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{K}</annotation></semantics></math> and bounded above and below by positive constants. Since
the perturbations tend to zero, the <math id="S6.p8.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and entropy convergence are
unchanged. The <math id="S6.p8.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> convergence also implies <math id="S6.p8.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> convergence here,
because all measures are supported in <math id="S6.p8.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex102" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex102.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}^{2}(p\,d\lambda,q\,d\lambda)\leq\frac{\operatorname{diam}(K)^{2}}{2}\|p-q\|_{L^{1}(K)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>diam</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}^{2}(p\,d\lambda,q\,d\lambda)\leq\frac{\operatorname{diam}(K)^{2}}{2}\|p-q\|_{L^{1}(K)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p8.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p8.4.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, on <math id="S6.p8.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex103" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
