<div id="S6.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p27.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p27.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S6.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is absolutely continuous, the quadratic optimal coupling
between <math id="S6.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p27.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is unique. Lemma
<a href="#S6.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 6.3. ‣ 6 A transport proof of the min–max entropy inequality ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> gives</span></p>
