<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting</h2>

<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1 </span>The four-function inequalities of Ahlswede–Daykin and Cordero-Erausquin–Maurey</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First we present a result of Cordero-Erausquin and Maurey <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, which may be thought of as a four-function variant
of the Prékopa–Leindler inequality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.1</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Cordero-Erausquin &amp; Maurey)<span id="S7.Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> be Borel measurable and integrable, and let <math id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. If</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex133" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex133.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(\lambda x+(1-\lambda)y)f_{4}((1-\lambda)x+\lambda y)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(\lambda x+(1-\lambda)y)f_{4}((1-\lambda)x+\lambda y),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex134" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex134.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int f_{1}\int f_{2}\leq\int f_{3}\int f_{4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int f_{1}\int f_{2}\leq\int f_{3}\int f_{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The preceding theorem is the specialization <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1/2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1-s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1-s</annotation></semantics></math> of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Proposition 4.6]</cite>, in the notation of that proposition;
also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Proposition 1.2]</cite> is the particular case with coefficients <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2/3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/3</annotation></semantics></math>. These results follow from the more general
linear-operator framework of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Theorem 1.6]</cite>. For example, the linear map in Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 7.1 (Cordero-Erausquin &amp; Maurey). ‣ 7.1 The four-function inequalities of Ahlswede–Daykin and Cordero-Erausquin–Maurey ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>
has block-matrix representation<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            Each scalar entry displayed in the matrix represents that scalar times the identity on <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span>
<math id="S7.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\begin{pmatrix}\lambda&amp;1-\lambda\\
1-\lambda&amp;\lambda\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>λ</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mi>λ</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\begin{pmatrix}\lambda&amp;1-\lambda\\
1-\lambda&amp;\lambda\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math>, as <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\begin{pmatrix}x\\
y\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}\lambda x+(1-\lambda)y\\
(1-\lambda)x+\lambda y\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>x</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>y</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\begin{pmatrix}x\\
y\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}\lambda x+(1-\lambda)y\\
(1-\lambda)x+\lambda y\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math>, with outputs corresponding to the inputs of <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In their paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, Cordero-Erausquin and Maurey ask whether there is a transport proof of the preceding theorem.
We give below a positive answer to this question, as part of a more general framework involving
entropy inequalities for coordinatewise power means under the quadratic
optimal transport coupling in <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The generalization that we pursue here concerns nonlinear means of <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, with the aim of connecting the Ahlswede–Daykin four-function theorem with that of Cordero-Erausquin and Maurey.
To this end recall the definition of the generalized mean from the introduction. For <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{R}\cup\{\pm\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{R}\cup\{\pm\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="S7.Ex135" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex135.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\alpha}^{\lambda}(x,y):=\left(\lambda x^{\alpha}+(1-\lambda)y^{\alpha}\right)^{\frac{1}{\alpha}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\alpha}^{\lambda}(x,y):=\left(\lambda x^{\alpha}+(1-\lambda)y^{\alpha}\right)^{\frac{1}{\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where the special cases <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\pm\infty,0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\pm\infty,0</annotation></semantics></math> are to be understood in the limit, namely
<math id="S7.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{-\infty}^{\lambda}(x,y)=\lim_{\alpha\to-\infty}M_{\alpha}^{\lambda}(x,y)=\min(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{-\infty}^{\lambda}(x,y)=\lim_{\alpha\to-\infty}M_{\alpha}^{\lambda}(x,y)=\min(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>, similarly
<math id="S7.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\infty}^{\lambda}(x,y)=\lim_{\alpha\to\infty}M_{\alpha}^{\lambda}(x,y)=\max(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\infty}^{\lambda}(x,y)=\lim_{\alpha\to\infty}M_{\alpha}^{\lambda}(x,y)=\max(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S7.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{0}^{\lambda}(x,y)=x^{\lambda}y^{1-\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{0}^{\lambda}(x,y)=x^{\lambda}y^{1-\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is the geometric mean. The case <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=-1</annotation></semantics></math> is the harmonic mean, while <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the arithmetic mean.
For vectors <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in(0,\infty)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in(0,\infty)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> we define a vector <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\alpha}^{\lambda}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\alpha}^{\lambda}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S7.Ex136" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex136.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{M}_{\alpha}^{\lambda}(x,y)_{i}={M}_{\alpha}^{\lambda}(x_{i},y_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{M}_{\alpha}^{\lambda}(x,y)_{i}={M}_{\alpha}^{\lambda}(x_{i},y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">With these definitions in place we can state the two theorems together.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.2</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Ahlswede &amp; Daykin, Cordero-Erausquin &amp; Maurey)<span id="S7.Thmthm2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:(0,\infty)^{d}\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:(0,\infty)^{d}\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> be integrable functions such that</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex137" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex137.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(M^{t}_{\alpha}(x,y))f_{4}(M_{\beta}^{1-t}(x,y))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>β</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(M^{t}_{\alpha}(x,y))f_{4}(M_{\beta}^{1-t}(x,y))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for <math id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\beta)=(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\beta)=(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\beta)=(-\infty,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\beta)=(-\infty,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex138" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex138.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int f_{1}\int f_{2}\leq\int f_{3}\int f_{4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int f_{1}\int f_{2}\leq\int f_{3}\int f_{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 7.3</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For either of the two parameter pairs in the theorem, restricting the
domain to <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,\infty)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,\infty)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> entails no loss relative to the corresponding
statement on <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. To see this, suppose that
<math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}\colon\mathbb{R}^{d}\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}\colon\mathbb{R}^{d}\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the pointwise hypothesis.
Let</p>
<table id="S7.Ex139" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex139.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{R}=[-R,R]^{d},\qquad a_{R}=(R+1)(1,\ldots,1)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{R}=[-R,R]^{d},\qquad a_{R}=(R+1)(1,\ldots,1),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and, for <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in(0,\infty)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in(0,\infty)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="S7.Ex140" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex140.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g_{i,R}(z)=f_{i}(z-a_{R})\mathbbm{1}_{Q_{R}}(z-a_{R}),\quad i=1,2,\qquad g_{i,R}(z)=f_{i}(z-a_{R}),\quad i=3,4." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i,R}(z)=f_{i}(z-a_{R})\mathbbm{1}_{Q_{R}}(z-a_{R}),\quad i=1,2,\qquad g_{i,R}(z)=f_{i}(z-a_{R}),\quad i=3,4.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The arithmetic-mean and min–max maps commute with common
translations. Hence the <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i,R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i,R}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the positive-orthant
hypothesis: if either translated input lies outside <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, its
left-hand side is zero, while if both lie in <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, both outputs remain
in <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{R}+Q_{R}\subset(0,\infty)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{R}+Q_{R}\subset(0,\infty)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and the original hypothesis applies.
The positive-orthant conclusion therefore gives</p>
<table id="S7.Ex141" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex141.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\int_{Q_{R}}f_{1}\right)\left(\int_{Q_{R}}f_{2}\right)\leq\left(\int_{(-a_{R},\infty)^{d}}f_{3}\right)\left(\int_{(-a_{R},\infty)^{d}}f_{4}\right)\leq\left(\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}f_{3}\right)\left(\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}f_{4}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\int_{Q_{R}}f_{1}\right)\left(\int_{Q_{R}}f_{2}\right)\leq\left(\int_{(-a_{R},\infty)^{d}}f_{3}\right)\left(\int_{(-a_{R},\infty)^{d}}f_{4}\right)\leq\left(\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}f_{3}\right)\left(\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}f_{4}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Letting <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and using monotone convergence proves the
full-space statement.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the sequel we consider the following question.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Question</span>: Are there other pairs of <math id="S7.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> such that Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 7.2 (Ahlswede &amp; Daykin, Cordero-Erausquin &amp; Maurey). ‣ 7.1 The four-function inequalities of Ahlswede–Daykin and Cordero-Erausquin–Maurey ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a> continues to hold as stated?</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To address this question, we first give a scalar criterion and its transport proof. We then obtain a weighted extension for orders in <math id="S7.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and the sharp range <math id="S7.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\alpha+\beta\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\alpha+\beta\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> when both means have equal weights.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2 </span>A scalar criterion and its transport proof</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, write</p>
<table id="S7.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{q,\theta}(x,y;z)=M_{q}^{\theta}(x,y)^{1-q}\left[\theta x^{q-1}+(1-\theta)y^{q-1}z\right],\qquad x,y,z&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{q,\theta}(x,y;z)=M_{q}^{\theta}(x,y)^{1-q}\left[\theta x^{q-1}+(1-\theta)y^{q-1}z\right],\qquad x,y,z&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{q,\theta}=\partial_{1}M_{q}^{\theta}+z\partial_{2}M_{q}^{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{q,\theta}=\partial_{1}M_{q}^{\theta}+z\partial_{2}M_{q}^{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>,
also when <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0</annotation></semantics></math>. The following determinant bound allows us to pass
from a scalar differential inequality to the transport Jacobians.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.4</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be a positive definite symmetric <math id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times d</annotation></semantics></math> matrix, and write</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex142" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex142.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=L\operatorname{diag}(\delta_{1},\ldots,\delta_{d})L^{\mathsf{T}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=L\operatorname{diag}(\delta_{1},\ldots,\delta_{d})L^{\mathsf{T}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is unit lower triangular and <math id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. For positive
diagonal matrices <math id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\operatorname{diag}(u_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\operatorname{diag}(u_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\operatorname{diag}(v_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\operatorname{diag}(v_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex143" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex143.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(U+VA)\geq\prod_{i=1}^{d}(u_{i}+v_{i}\delta_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(U+VA)\geq\prod_{i=1}^{d}(u_{i}+v_{i}\delta_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\subseteq\{1,\ldots,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\subseteq\{1,\ldots,d\}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> be the corresponding
principal submatrix. Cauchy–Binet gives</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex144" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex144.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det A_{I}=\sum_{|J|=|I|}(\det L_{I,J})^{2}\prod_{j\in J}\delta_{j}\geq\prod_{i\in I}\delta_{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det A_{I}=\sum_{|J|=|I|}(\det L_{I,J})^{2}\prod_{j\in J}\delta_{j}\geq\prod_{i\in I}\delta_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">because the summand with <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=I</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det L_{I,I}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det L_{I,I}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Expanding the determinant by multilinearity now gives</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex145" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex145.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(U+VA)=\sum_{I}\det A_{I}\prod_{i\in I}v_{i}\prod_{i\notin I}u_{i}\geq\prod_{i=1}^{d}(u_{i}+v_{i}\delta_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>I</mi></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(U+VA)=\sum_{I}\det A_{I}\prod_{i\in I}v_{i}\prod_{i\notin I}u_{i}\geq\prod_{i=1}^{d}(u_{i}+v_{i}\delta_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">Here the empty principal minor and empty products are understood to
equal one.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We will also use the following compact-support form of the Gibbs
variational formula.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.5</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Restricted Gibbs variational formula)<span id="S7.Thmthm5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon(0,\infty)^{d}\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon(0,\infty)^{d}\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> be measurable with
<math id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\int_{(0,\infty)^{d}}f\,d\lambda&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.111em">&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\int_{(0,\infty)^{d}}f\,d\lambda&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S7.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\log\int_{(0,\infty)^{d}}f\,d\lambda=\sup_{\nu}\left\{\int\log f\,d\nu-H(\nu\mid\lambda)\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mi>f</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mi>ν</mi></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\int_{(0,\infty)^{d}}f\,d\lambda=\sup_{\nu}\left\{\int\log f\,d\nu-H(\nu\mid\lambda)\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the supremum may be restricted to absolutely continuous
probability measures with compact support and finite entropy that are
concentrated on <math id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{f&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math> and satisfy <math id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log f\in L^{1}(\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log f\in L^{1}(\nu)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The Gibbs variational formula gives the upper bound. For the reverse
bound, set</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex146" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex146.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{k}=\{x\in[k^{-1},k]^{d}:k^{-1}\leq f(x)\leq k\},\qquad Z_{k}=\int_{E_{k}}f\,d\lambda." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{k}=\{x\in[k^{-1},k]^{d}:k^{-1}\leq f(x)\leq k\},\qquad Z_{k}=\int_{E_{k}}f\,d\lambda.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">The sets <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> increase to <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{0&lt;f&lt;\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0&lt;f&lt;\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math>, up to a null set, so
<math id="S7.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{k}\uparrow\int f\,d\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↑</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{k}\uparrow\int f\,d\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Whenever <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, define</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex147" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex147.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\nu_{k}=Z_{k}^{-1}f\mathbbm{1}_{E_{k}}\,d\lambda." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\nu_{k}=Z_{k}^{-1}f\mathbbm{1}_{E_{k}}\,d\lambda.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">Then <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is admissible and a direct calculation gives</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex148" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex148.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\log f\,d\nu_{k}-H(\nu_{k}\mid\lambda)=\log Z_{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\log f\,d\nu_{k}-H(\nu_{k}\mid\lambda)=\log Z_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">Letting <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> proves the claim.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.6</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(A scalar criterion for power-mean inequalities)<span id="S7.Thmthm6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r,s,t\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r,s,t\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and set
<math id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=rs+(1-r)t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=rs+(1-r)t</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that</span></p>
<table id="S7.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\alpha,s}(x,y;z)^{r}D_{\beta,t}(x,y;z)^{1-r}\geq z^{1-m}\qquad\text{for all }x,y,z&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\alpha,s}(x,y;z)^{r}D_{\beta,t}(x,y;z)^{1-r}\geq z^{1-m}\qquad\text{for all }x,y,z&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{1},\nu_{2}\in\mathcal{P}_{2}((0,\infty)^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{1},\nu_{2}\in\mathcal{P}_{2}((0,\infty)^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> be absolutely
continuous probability measures with finite entropy relative to
Lebesgue measure <math id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, and let
<math id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=(\operatorname{Id},T)_{\#}\nu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Id</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=(\operatorname{Id},T)_{\#}\nu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be their Brenier coupling. Define</span></p>
