<div id="S7.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The maps <math id="S7.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{3},G_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{3},G_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> are injective. Indeed, positive definiteness of
<math id="S7.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="DT" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">DT</annotation></semantics></math> and convexity of <math id="S7.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> imply strict monotonicity of <math id="S7.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S7.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{j}(x)=G_{j}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{j}(x)=G_{j}(y)</annotation></semantics></math>, strict increase of each scalar mean in both
arguments gives</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex153" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex153.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i}-y_{i})(T_{i}(x)-T_{i}(y))\leq 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i}-y_{i})(T_{i}(x)-T_{i}(y))\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">in every coordinate. Summing contradicts strict monotonicity unless
<math id="S7.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=y</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the change-of-variables formulas give</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex154" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex154.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H(\nu_{j}\mid\lambda)=H(\nu_{1}\mid\lambda)-\int\log\det DG_{j}\,d\nu_{1},\qquad j=3,4," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>D</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\nu_{j}\mid\lambda)=H(\nu_{1}\mid\lambda)-\int\log\det DG_{j}\,d\nu_{1},\qquad j=3,4,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">and</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex155" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex155.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H(\nu_{2}\mid\lambda)=H(\nu_{1}\mid\lambda)-\int\log\det DT\,d\nu_{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\nu_{2}\mid\lambda)=H(\nu_{1}\mid\lambda)-\int\log\det DT\,d\nu_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p7.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p7.4.1" class="ltx_text">Taking logarithms in (<a href="#S7.E27" title="In Proof. ‣ 7.2 A scalar criterion and its transport proof ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>),
integrating, and using these identities proves
(<a href="#S7.E24" title="In Theorem 7.6 (A scalar criterion for power-mean inequalities). ‣ 7.2 A scalar criterion and its transport proof ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>) in the regular case.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S7.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Approximation.</em><span id="S7.SS2.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text"> We now remove the regularity assumptions. By
(<a href="#S6.E17" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ 6 A transport proof of the min–max entropy inequality ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>), the input measures have finite
entropy relative to the standard Gaussian. The construction in
Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 6.2. ‣ 6 A transport proof of the min–max entropy inequality ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a> can be carried out
inside the positive orthant, using the common exhaustion</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex156" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex156.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{R}=\left\{x\in(0,\infty)^{d}:\sum_{i=1}^{d}(x_{i}+x_{i}^{-1})&lt;R\right\},\qquad R&gt;2d." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{R}=\left\{x\in(0,\infty)^{d}:\sum_{i=1}^{d}(x_{i}+x_{i}^{-1})&lt;R\right\},\qquad R&gt;2d.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">These domains are bounded, smooth and uniformly convex: the defining
function has positive definite Hessian, and its level sets are compact
and regular. First restrict and normalize on a smaller member of the
exhaustion. Truncate the resulting densities from above, mollify
within the larger domain, and mix with a vanishing uniform density
there. Choosing each error successively as in the cited lemma gives
densities smooth on the closures and bounded above and below by
positive constants on common domains, such that</span></p>
<table id="S7.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{i}^{k}\longrightarrow\nu_{i}\quad\text{in }W_{2},\qquad H(\nu_{i}^{k}\mid\lambda)\longrightarrow H(\nu_{i}\mid\lambda),\quad i=1,2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>in </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{i}^{k}\longrightarrow\nu_{i}\quad\text{in }W_{2},\qquad H(\nu_{i}^{k}\mid\lambda)\longrightarrow H(\nu_{i}\mid\lambda),\quad i=1,2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">The entropy convergence follows from the Gaussian entropy convergence
in that lemma and the second-moment identity
(<a href="#S6.E17" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ 6 A transport proof of the min–max entropy inequality ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S7.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Brenier coupling between <math id="S7.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{1}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{1}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 6.3. ‣ 6 A transport proof of the min–max entropy inequality ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> gives
<math id="S7.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{k}\to\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{k}\to\pi</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S7.SS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Every power mean of finite real order with
weights in <math id="S7.SS2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> extends continuously to the closed positive
orthant and lies between the coordinatewise minimum and maximum.
Consequently its growth is at most linear, and</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex157" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex157.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(M_{\alpha}^{s})_{\#}\pi_{k}\longrightarrow\nu_{3},\qquad(M_{\beta}^{t})_{\#}\pi_{k}\longrightarrow\nu_{4}\quad\text{in }W_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>#</mi></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>#</mi></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mtext>in </mtext><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M_{\alpha}^{s})_{\#}\pi_{k}\longrightarrow\nu_{3},\qquad(M_{\beta}^{t})_{\#}\pi_{k}\longrightarrow\nu_{4}\quad\text{in }W_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">Entropy relative to Lebesgue measure is lower semicontinuous under
<math id="S7.SS2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> convergence, by (<a href="#S6.E17" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ 6 A transport proof of the min–max entropy inequality ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) and
convergence of second moments. The same identity bounds the output
entropies below uniformly. We may therefore pass to the limit in the
weighted entropy inequality, proving
(<a href="#S7.E24" title="In Theorem 7.6 (A scalar criterion for power-mean inequalities). ‣ 7.2 A scalar criterion and its transport proof ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S7.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">The functional inequality.</em><span id="S7.SS2.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text">
Write <math id="S7.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=(0,\infty)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=(0,\infty)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is enough to consider the case when
<math id="S7.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\int_{Q}f_{i}\,d\lambda&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.111em">&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>Q</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\int_{Q}f_{i}\,d\lambda&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.SS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2,3,4\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2,3,4\}</annotation></semantics></math>. If either
input integral vanishes the conclusion is immediate. If both input
integrals are positive, fix <math id="S7.SS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y\in\{f_{2}&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\{f_{2}&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math> and a positive-measure
subset <math id="S7.SS2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S7.SS2.p10.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{f_{1}&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{1}&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math> contained in a compact box <math id="S7.SS2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subset Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subset Q</annotation></semantics></math>.
For this fixed <math id="S7.SS2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, each map <math id="S7.SS2.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\mapsto M_{q}^{\theta}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\mapsto M_{q}^{\theta}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> is
bi-Lipschitz on <math id="S7.SS2.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S7.SS2.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>: its
coordinate derivatives are continuous and strictly positive there.
The pointwise hypothesis forces both <math id="S7.SS2.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> to be positive
on the respective images of <math id="S7.SS2.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, which have positive measure.
Thus both output integrals are positive as well.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S7.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{1},\nu_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{1},\nu_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be absolutely continuous probability measures with
compact support and finite entropy, chosen so that <math id="S7.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is
concentrated on <math id="S7.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{f_{i}&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{i}&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log f_{i}\in L^{1}(\nu_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log f_{i}\in L^{1}(\nu_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, for
<math id="S7.SS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S7.SS2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> be their Brenier coupling, and define</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex158" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex158.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{3}=(m_{3})_{\#}\pi,\qquad\nu_{4}=(m_{4})_{\#}\pi," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{3}=(m_{3})_{\#}\pi,\qquad\nu_{4}=(m_{4})_{\#}\pi,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">where</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex159" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex159.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{3}(x,y)=M_{\alpha}^{s}(x,y),\qquad m_{4}(x,y)=M_{\beta}^{t}(x,y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{3}(x,y)=M_{\alpha}^{s}(x,y),\qquad m_{4}(x,y)=M_{\beta}^{t}(x,y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the entropy inequality just proved,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex160" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex160.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="rH(\nu_{3}\mid\lambda)+(1-r)H(\nu_{4}\mid\lambda)\leq mH(\nu_{1}\mid\lambda)+(1-m)H(\nu_{2}\mid\lambda)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rH(\nu_{3}\mid\lambda)+(1-r)H(\nu_{4}\mid\lambda)\leq mH(\nu_{1}\mid\lambda)+(1-m)H(\nu_{2}\mid\lambda).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">The logarithmic integrals below are well defined. Indeed, the output
measures have compact support and finite entropy, so the Gibbs bound
on a compact set, applied to <math id="S7.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\{\log(1+f_{j}),k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\{\log(1+f_{j}),k\}</annotation></semantics></math> and then letting <math id="S7.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, makes their positive logarithmic parts integrable. The pointwise hypothesis and the
integrability of <math id="S7.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log f_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log f_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> then give integrability of
the negative parts.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Taking logarithms in
(<a href="#S7.E25" title="In Theorem 7.6 (A scalar criterion for power-mean inequalities). ‣ 7.2 A scalar criterion and its transport proof ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>) and integrating against
<math id="S7.SS2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> gives</span></p>
<table id="S7.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
