<div id="S7.SS2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S7.SS2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Necessity of the scalar condition.</em><span id="S7.SS2.p14.1.2" class="ltx_text"> Fix <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y,z&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y,z&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and take the input measures to be uniform
on</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex165" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex165.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\varepsilon}=[x-\varepsilon,x+\varepsilon],\qquad J_{\varepsilon}=[y-z\varepsilon,y+z\varepsilon]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\varepsilon}=[x-\varepsilon,x+\varepsilon],\qquad J_{\varepsilon}=[y-z\varepsilon,y+z\varepsilon],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is small enough that both intervals are
positive. Their Brenier map is <math id="S7.SS2.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(u)=y+z(u-x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(u)=y+z(u-x)</annotation></semantics></math>. The
change-of-variables formulas reduce the entropy assertion to</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex166" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
