<div id="S7.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, the weighted
arithmetic-geometric mean inequality gives</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex168" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex168.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{q,\theta}(x,y;z)\geq M_{q}^{\theta}(x,y)^{1-q}(x^{\theta}y^{1-\theta})^{q-1}z^{1-\theta}\geq z^{1-\theta}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>θ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{q,\theta}(x,y;z)\geq M_{q}^{\theta}(x,y)^{1-q}(x^{\theta}y^{1-\theta})^{q-1}z^{1-\theta}\geq z^{1-\theta},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">because <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{q}^{\theta}(x,y)\geq x^{\theta}y^{1-\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>θ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{q}^{\theta}(x,y)\geq x^{\theta}y^{1-\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-q\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-q\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Apply Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 7.6 (A scalar criterion for power-mean inequalities). ‣ 7.2 A scalar criterion and its transport proof ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.6</span></a> with
<math id="S7.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\beta=q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\beta=q</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=t=\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=t=\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1/2</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain
(<a href="#S7.E31" title="In Corollary 7.7 (Entropy inequality for coordinatewise power means). ‣ 7.3 Power-mean consequences ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>). The cases <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=0,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=0,1</annotation></semantics></math>
are immediate. Applying this inequality to the two output means,
multiplying by <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-r</annotation></semantics></math>, and adding proves
(<a href="#S7.E32" title="In Corollary 7.7 (Entropy inequality for coordinatewise power means). ‣ 7.3 Power-mean consequences ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 7.8</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Weighted four-function inequality)<span id="S7.Thmthm8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r,s,t\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r,s,t\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S7.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=sr+(1-r)t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=sr+(1-r)t</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S7.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. If integrable functions
<math id="S7.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}\colon(0,\infty)^{d}\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}\colon(0,\infty)^{d}\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy
(<a href="#S7.E25" title="In Theorem 7.6 (A scalar criterion for power-mean inequalities). ‣ 7.2 A scalar criterion and its transport proof ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>) for all <math id="S7.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in(0,\infty)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in(0,\infty)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, then
(<a href="#S7.E26" title="In Theorem 7.6 (A scalar criterion for power-mean inequalities). ‣ 7.2 A scalar criterion and its transport proof ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>) holds.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The bound in the proof of Corollary <a href="#S7.Thmthm7" title="Corollary 7.7 (Entropy inequality for coordinatewise power means). ‣ 7.3 Power-mean consequences ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.7</span></a> gives</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex169" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex169.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\alpha,s}(x,y;z)^{r}D_{\beta,t}(x,y;z)^{1-r}\geq z^{r(1-s)+(1-r)(1-t)}=z^{1-m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\alpha,s}(x,y;z)^{r}D_{\beta,t}(x,y;z)^{1-r}\geq z^{r(1-s)+(1-r)(1-t)}=z^{1-m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">The conclusion follows from Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 7.6 (A scalar criterion for power-mean inequalities). ‣ 7.2 A scalar criterion and its transport proof ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.6</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 7.9</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Observe that taking <math id="S7.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1-t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1-t</annotation></semantics></math> forces <math id="S7.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and we see that Corollary <a href="#S7.Thmthm8" title="Corollary 7.8 (Weighted four-function inequality). ‣ 7.3 Power-mean consequences ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.8</span></a> extends Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 7.2 (Ahlswede &amp; Daykin, Cordero-Erausquin &amp; Maurey). ‣ 7.1 The four-function inequalities of Ahlswede–Daykin and Cordero-Erausquin–Maurey ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a> to include <math id="S7.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\beta)\in[0,1]\times[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\beta)\in[0,1]\times[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that taking <math id="S7.Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, one recovers a generalization of the usual three-function Prékopa–Leindler inequality.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 7.10</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note also that, in Slomka <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, Theorem 1.2]</cite>, a similar result for <math id="S7.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained in the discrete setting, but with exponents
<math id="S7.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2},\alpha_{3},\alpha_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2},\alpha_{3},\alpha_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to <math id="S7.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1},f_{2},f_{3},f_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1},f_{2},f_{3},f_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S7.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max(\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2})\leq\min(\alpha_{3},\alpha_{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max(\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2})\leq\min(\alpha_{3},\alpha_{4})</annotation></semantics></math>, which, for us, would force <math id="S7.Thmthm10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=r=1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=r=1/2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Owing to the continuous setting’s simpler computations we attain a more general formulation here and an interpolation between the usual three-function and a four-function variant of Prékopa–Leindler.
In the discrete setting three-function versions of Prékopa–Leindler inequalities seem to be more delicate, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> for a partial result on <math id="S7.Thmthm10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>
as well as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> which reduces the functional problem to a geometric question.
For a four-function version of the Prékopa–Leindler inequality
on <math id="S7.Thmthm10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> we refer the reader to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 7.11</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Equal-weight power means)<span id="S7.Thmthm11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{1},\nu_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{1},\nu_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> be as in
Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 7.6 (A scalar criterion for power-mean inequalities). ‣ 7.2 A scalar criterion and its transport proof ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.6</span></a>. If
<math id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\alpha+\beta\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\alpha+\beta\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S7.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H((M_{\alpha}^{1/2})_{\#}\pi\mid\lambda)+H((M_{\beta}^{1/2})_{\#}\pi\mid\lambda)\leq H(\nu_{1}\mid\lambda)+H(\nu_{2}\mid\lambda)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>β</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>#</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H((M_{\alpha}^{1/2})_{\#}\pi\mid\lambda)+H((M_{\beta}^{1/2})_{\#}\pi\mid\lambda)\leq H(\nu_{1}\mid\lambda)+H(\nu_{2}\mid\lambda).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
