<p id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, if nonnegative integrable functions on <math id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,\infty)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,\infty)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S7.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(M_{\alpha}^{1/2}(x,y))f_{4}(M_{\beta}^{1/2}(x,y))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>β</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(x)f_{2}(y)\leq f_{3}(M_{\alpha}^{1/2}(x,y))f_{4}(M_{\beta}^{1/2}(x,y)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then</span></p>
<table id="S7.E35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\int f_{1}\right)\left(\int f_{2}\right)\leq\left(\int f_{3}\right)\left(\int f_{4}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\int f_{1}\right)\left(\int f_{2}\right)\leq\left(\int f_{3}\right)\left(\int f_{4}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For finite orders, the range <math id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\alpha+\beta\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\alpha+\beta\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> is necessary
for either assertion to hold for all admissible inputs, already in
dimension one. Both assertions also hold for the limiting pairs
<math id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\beta)=(-\infty,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\beta)=(-\infty,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\infty,-\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\infty,-\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Take <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=s=t=1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=s=t=1/2</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 7.6 (A scalar criterion for power-mean inequalities). ‣ 7.2 A scalar criterion and its transport proof ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.6</span></a>.
For fixed <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, put</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex170" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex170.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{q}=\partial_{1}M_{q}^{1/2}(x,y),\qquad b_{q}=\partial_{2}M_{q}^{1/2}(x,y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>q</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>q</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{q}=\partial_{1}M_{q}^{1/2}(x,y),\qquad b_{q}=\partial_{2}M_{q}^{1/2}(x,y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since these derivatives are positive,</span></p>
<table id="S7.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.Ex171"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex171.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\inf_{z&gt;0}\frac{D_{\alpha,1/2}(x,y;z)D_{\beta,1/2}(x,y;z)}{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">inf</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>z</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\inf_{z&gt;0}\frac{D_{\alpha,1/2}(x,y;z)D_{\beta,1/2}(x,y;z)}{z}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex171.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\inf_{z&gt;0}\left[\frac{a_{\alpha}a_{\beta}}{z}+a_{\alpha}b_{\beta}+b_{\alpha}a_{\beta}+b_{\alpha}b_{\beta}z\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><mi>z</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\inf_{z&gt;0}\left[\frac{a_{\alpha}a_{\beta}}{z}+a_{\alpha}b_{\beta}+b_{\alpha}a_{\beta}+b_{\alpha}b_{\beta}z\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.Ex172"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex172.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left(\sqrt{a_{\alpha}b_{\beta}}+\sqrt{b_{\alpha}a_{\beta}}\right)^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left(\sqrt{a_{\alpha}b_{\beta}}+\sqrt{b_{\alpha}a_{\beta}}\right)^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">The derivatives are homogeneous of degree zero, so normalize <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xy=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xy=1</annotation></semantics></math>
and write <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=e^{-u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=e^{-u}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=e^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=e^{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. Substitution shows that the logarithm
of the last expression is</span></p>
<table id="S7.E36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\alpha,\beta}(u)=\frac{1-\alpha}{\alpha}\log\cosh(\alpha u)+\frac{1-\beta}{\beta}\log\cosh(\beta u)+2\log\cosh\left(\frac{\alpha-\beta}{2}u\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mi>α</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\alpha,\beta}(u)=\frac{1-\alpha}{\alpha}\log\cosh(\alpha u)+\frac{1-\beta}{\beta}\log\cosh(\beta u)+2\log\cosh\left(\frac{\alpha-\beta}{2}u\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">The terms at zero order are interpreted by continuity. Thus the
scalar condition is equivalent to <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\alpha,\beta}(u)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\alpha,\beta}(u)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>
for all <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix <math id="S7.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and set</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex173" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex173.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(v)=\frac{\log\cosh(vu)}{v},\qquad g(0)=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>v</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(v)=\frac{\log\cosh(vu)}{v},\qquad g(0)=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">This function is odd, and is increasing and concave on <math id="S7.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Indeed, writing <math id="S7.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=uv</annotation></semantics></math>, one has</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex174" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex174.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{2}g^{\prime}(v)=w\tanh w-\log\cosh w\geq 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{2}g^{\prime}(v)=w\tanh w-\log\cosh w\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">and</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex175" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex175.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{3}g^{\prime\prime}(v)=w^{2}\operatorname{sech}^{2}w-2w\tanh w+2\log\cosh w\leq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>sech</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{3}g^{\prime\prime}(v)=w^{2}\operatorname{sech}^{2}w-2w\tanh w+2\log\cosh w\leq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">Both expressions on the right vanish at <math id="S7.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and their derivatives
are respectively <math id="S7.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\operatorname{sech}^{2}w\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>sech</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\operatorname{sech}^{2}w\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S7.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2w^{2}\operatorname{sech}^{2}w\tanh w\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>sech</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2w^{2}\operatorname{sech}^{2}w\tanh w\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Concavity and <math id="S7.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math> also imply subadditivity on <math id="S7.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">By symmetry in the orders, write
<math id="S7.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=c+h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=c+h</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=c-h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=c-h</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq c\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq c\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then (<a href="#S7.E36" title="In Proof. ‣ 7.3 Power-mean consequences ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>) becomes</span></p>
<table id="S7.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.Ex176"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex176.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K_{\alpha,\beta}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K_{\alpha,\beta}(u)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex176.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(1-c)\bigl[g(c+h)+g(c-h)\bigr]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(1-c)\bigl[g(c+h)+g(c-h)\bigr]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.Ex177"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex177.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+h\bigl[2g(h)+g(c-h)-g(c+h)\bigr]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+h\bigl[2g(h)+g(c-h)-g(c+h)\bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">The first bracket is nonnegative by monotonicity and oddness.
If <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\geq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\geq c</annotation></semantics></math>, concavity gives
<math id="S7.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g(h)\geq g(h-c)+g(h+c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g(h)\geq g(h-c)+g(h+c)</annotation></semantics></math>, so the second bracket is also
nonnegative. If <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\leq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\leq c</annotation></semantics></math>, subadditivity and concavity give</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex178" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex178.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(c+h)\leq g(c-h)+g(2h)\leq g(c-h)+2g(h)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(c+h)\leq g(c-h)+g(2h)\leq g(c-h)+2g(h),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">with the same conclusion. Since <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\alpha,\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\alpha,\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> is even in <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>,
this proves the scalar condition for all <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and hence both
assertions for finite orders.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">For necessity, writing <math id="S7.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\alpha+\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\alpha+\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, expansion at the origin gives</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex179" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex179.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\alpha,\beta}(u)=\frac{S(2-S)}{4}u^{2}+O(u^{4})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\alpha,\beta}(u)=\frac{S(2-S)}{4}u^{2}+O(u^{4}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S7.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\notin[0,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\notin[0,2]</annotation></semantics></math>, the scalar condition fails for small nonzero <math id="S7.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>.
The necessity assertion in
Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 7.6 (A scalar criterion for power-mean inequalities). ‣ 7.2 A scalar criterion and its transport proof ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.6</span></a> proves sharpness.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, let <math id="S7.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\to-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\to-\infty</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1-\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1-\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. The two
means converge pointwise to the coordinatewise minimum and maximum.
They are bounded in norm by <math id="S7.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|x|+|y|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|x|+|y|</annotation></semantics></math>, so dominated convergence
under the fixed coupling <math id="S7.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> gives <math id="S7.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> convergence of their
marginals. Lower semicontinuity, with the same Gaussian entropy
identity used above, gives the limiting entropy inequality.
The Gibbs argument in the proof of
Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 3.2 (Batty-Bollmann []). ‣ 3 The Euclidean Ahlswede–Daykin 4-function theorem ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> applies to this inequality
and gives the functional endpoint; no continuity of the <math id="S7.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is
needed. The other limiting pair follows by exchanging the outputs.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 7.12</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,Y</annotation></semantics></math> be random vectors taking values in <math id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,\infty)^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,\infty)^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, with
absolutely continuous laws, finite second moments, and finite Shannon
entropies, coupled by quadratic optimal transport. The means below act
coordinatewise. In terms of Shannon entropy,
Corollary <a href="#S7.Thmthm11" title="Corollary 7.11 (Equal-weight power means). ‣ 7.3 Power-mean consequences ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.11</span></a> gives</p>
<table id="S7.Ex180" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex180.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(M_{\alpha}^{1/2}(X,Y))+H(M_{\beta}^{1/2}(X,Y))\geq H(X)+H(Y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>β</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(M_{\alpha}^{1/2}(X,Y))+H(M_{\beta}^{1/2}(X,Y))\geq H(X)+H(Y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.2" class="ltx_p">whenever <math id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\alpha+\beta\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\alpha+\beta\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Equivalently,</p>
<table id="S7.Ex181" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex181.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(M_{\alpha}^{1/2}(X,Y))N(M_{\beta}^{1/2}(X,Y))\geq N(X)N(Y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>β</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(M_{\alpha}^{1/2}(X,Y))N(M_{\beta}^{1/2}(X,Y))\geq N(X)N(Y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.3" class="ltx_p">More generally, under the scalar condition
(<a href="#S7.E23" title="In Theorem 7.6 (A scalar criterion for power-mean inequalities). ‣ 7.2 A scalar criterion and its transport proof ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>),
Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 7.6 (A scalar criterion for power-mean inequalities). ‣ 7.2 A scalar criterion and its transport proof ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.6</span></a> gives the weighted inequality</p>
<table id="S7.Ex182" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex182.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(M_{\alpha}^{s}(X,Y))^{r}N(M_{\beta}^{t}(X,Y))^{1-r}\geq N(X)^{m}N(Y)^{1-m},\qquad m=rs+(1-r)t," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(M_{\alpha}^{s}(X,Y))^{r}N(M_{\beta}^{t}(X,Y))^{1-r}\geq N(X)^{m}N(Y)^{1-m},\qquad m=rs+(1-r)t,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.4" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r,s,t\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r,s,t\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. For a single mean,
Corollary <a href="#S7.Thmthm7" title="Corollary 7.7 (Entropy inequality for coordinatewise power means). ‣ 7.3 Power-mean consequences ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.7</span></a> gives</p>
<table id="S7.Ex183" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex183.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(M_{q}^{\theta}(X,Y))\geq N(X)^{\theta}N(Y)^{1-\theta},\qquad q,\theta\in[0,1]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(M_{q}^{\theta}(X,Y))\geq N(X)^{\theta}N(Y)^{1-\theta},\qquad q,\theta\in[0,1].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.5" class="ltx_p">As in Remark <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Remark 3.4. ‣ 3 The Euclidean Ahlswede–Daykin 4-function theorem ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, these are multiplicative
entropy-power inequalities under the optimal transport coupling,
rather than the independent coupling. The min–max limit of the
equal-weight inequality recovers the inequality in that remark for
random vectors in the positive orthant.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 7.13</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The equal-weight result includes the complementary orders
<math id="S7.Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,1-q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,1-q)</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S7.Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. The choice <math id="S7.Thmthm13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\beta)=(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\beta)=(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>
gives the midpoint case of the Cordero-Erausquin–Maurey inequality,
while the min–max limit gives Ahlswede–Daykin. The full weighted
Cordero-Erausquin–Maurey result follows instead from
Corollary <a href="#S7.Thmthm7" title="Corollary 7.7 (Entropy inequality for coordinatewise power means). ‣ 7.3 Power-mean consequences ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.7</span></a> with
<math id="S7.Thmthm13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 7.14</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The orders may also vary with the coordinate. For a vector
<math id="S7.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=(q_{1},\ldots,q_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=(q_{1},\ldots,q_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, define
<math id="S7.Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{q}^{\theta}(x,y)_{i}=M_{q_{i}}^{\theta}(x_{i},y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{q}^{\theta}(x,y)_{i}=M_{q_{i}}^{\theta}(x_{i},y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The determinant proof applies whenever
(<a href="#S7.E23" title="In Theorem 7.6 (A scalar criterion for power-mean inequalities). ‣ 7.2 A scalar criterion and its transport proof ‣ 7 Connections with Prékopa–Leindler in the continuous setting ‣ Four Function Theorems Through Optimal Transport and Entropic Analogs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) holds in each coordinate, with
the same <math id="S7.Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r,s,t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r,s,t</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the weighted result allows
<math id="S7.Thmthm14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i},\beta_{i}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i},\beta_{i}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, and the equal-weight result allows
<math id="S7.Thmthm14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\alpha_{i}+\beta_{i}\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\alpha_{i}+\beta_{i}\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> in every coordinate.</p>
</div>
</div>
